ession than  a handcrafted neural network or even a  gradient boosting classifier so  we are keeping that aside if these  blackboard concerns arrives arise and  the random forest was interestingly  regularly overfitting on our data  for our hyperparameter tuning we used  hyper opt a very nice package that i  would highly recommend  yes and  i think i have to speed up a bit and our  resulting model performances were up to  90 percent roc aoc  and  on our test set  and to  estimate our  generalization performance or our  variance of  our possible generalization performance  we did the following  after our modeling phase was  finished we  we let the random seed lose  so to say  so we actually  evaluated our models  with  a multitude of 90 10  test split train test splits and then we  measured the the scores on the test sets  and printed them here and so  these this box plot on the very left  side  shows that one of our models had a great  a  high variance  depending on how we did the train test  split this was um  we were not happy to see this of course  but  now i can say that  we um  we got another data set for evaluation  from a very different kind from another  con  continent and a very different  population  and all the models that  were trained on all the different 90  training sets  all  even evaluated  very very  strong and very good on this external  data set so we are thinking  we think that  through the manual transcription of the  clinical systems into the excel sheet  there was a lot of noise introduced  maybe there's there was also before a  lot of noise in our in our labels this  is um  clint so as the clinicians or the  medical world says real world data  but yeah seeing that we are so that we  are well performing on an external data  set  that was actually gathered from a  prospective study was very very nice and  relieving so what our next steps in  terms of modeling  um we are planning to do a probability  calibration and for that we are  extracting 